WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.575
CI/CD compromise can turn the build pipeline into a path for unauthorized payment application changes.

2
00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:13.969
Source control permissions, runner environments, deployment tokens, and artifact repositories all need protection.

3
00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:20.843
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software is authentic and unchanged.

4
00:00:20.843 --> 00:00:25.483
Integrity verification helps protect the path from source to build to release.

5
00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:32.587
Insecure deserialization occurs when applications trust serialized data to reconstruct objects or behavior.

6
00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:40.450
Attackers may manipulate serialized input to alter state, bypass controls, or trigger unsafe server behavior.

7
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:49.489
Build pipeline attacks are especially relevant when deployment automation can affect payment pages, scripts, APIs, or administrative tools.

8
00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:53.710
Change traceability, approvals, and monitoring help reduce this risk.

9
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.684
Use code signing and artifact integrity checks.

10
00:00:56.684 --> 00:01:00.745
Secure CI/CD pipelines with least privilege and approvals.

11
00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:03.638
Monitor and log system and application changes.

12
